🔁 Move window right → Remove 4, Add 7

               ↓   ↓   ↓
Index:     0   1   2   3   4   5   6
Array:    [4,  2,  1,  7,  8,  1,  2]
Window:        [2, 1, 7] → sum = 10

🔁 Move again → Remove 2, Add 8

                   ↓   ↓   ↓
Index:     0   1   2   3   4   5   6
Array:    [4,  2,  1,  7,  8,  1,  2]
Window:            [1, 7, 8] → sum = 16

🔁 And so on...

🌀 Real-Life Analogy: Spotify Playlist Skipping
Imagine you're reviewing a playlist of songs:

You only want to listen to 3 at a time

Every few minutes, you skip to the next 3

But you don't start over; you just slide the group forward by 1

That’s fixed-size sliding window — your “now playing” panel is like the window.

String: "a b c a b b"
Index:   0 1 2 3 4 5

Start: left = 0, right = 0 → Add 'a'
Window: [a]

→ Add 'b'
Window: [a b]

→ Add 'c'
Window: [a b c] → ❌ Now we have 3 unique → Too many!

→ Shrink from left → Remove 'a'
Window: [b c]

→ Still valid (2 unique)
→ Add 'a'
Window: [b c a] → ❌ Again 3 unique → Shrink from left
This is variable window:

Keep expanding while condition is valid

Shrink from left when it breaks

Track the best length along the way

🤯 In Real Life: Browsing Apps with Limited RAM
You open tabs (expand window)

If memory is full (too many), close older tabs (shrink window)

Always trying to maximize active tabs without crossing limit

That's a variable-size sliding window!

[4, 2, 1, 7, 8]     →  size = 3
 ^     ^
start  end          → just slides right step by step

[a, b, c, a, b, b]  → max 2 unique
 ^           ^
start      end      → dynamically expands/shrinks
